  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 52-year-old woman with type II achalasia underwent laparoscopic Heller myotomy with Dor fundoplication 3 years ago, with good initial symptom relief. Over the past 6 months she has developed progressive dysphagia to solids and liquids, with occasional regurgitation. Timed barium swallow shows a column height of 8cm at 5 minutes (improved from a pre-operative 12cm), and high-resolution manometry confirms persistent absence of peristalsis with an elevated integrated relaxation pressure (IRP) of 22mmHg. Upper GI endoscopy excludes stricture or malignancy at the gastro-oesophageal junction.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['recurrent achalasia symptoms','post-Heller myotomy failure','POEM']
),
('d4000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 'mrcp2', '5094d3e0-ad2e-4496-b90d-d6633dcb6866',
  'draft', 'advanced',
